{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787123982,"nonce":"rJhheYNUc+qFM5bZVjNxoGL8ttQ=","title":"**What is wrong.** `unacked_reminder` is to be removed. The owner ruled it on 2026-08-19, in the same breath as `frozen` (`e5c060b`) and the one-case-per-conversation rule that re-scopes `7570090`. It is not a defect being fixed; it is a feature being withdrawn, and the reason to file it rather than fold it into `7570090` is that its removal DISSOLVES a hazard that ticket would otherwise have to solve.","message":"**The demonstration.** The hazard is written into the code that implements it. `internal/notification/repository/reminders.go:115-127` documents the once-per-generation LATCH — a `NOT EXISTS` clause keyed on the literal pair `(alert_group, group_id)` — and then states its own failure mode in the imperative:\n\n\u003e ⚠️ MOVING `unacked_reminder` TO ANOTHER SUBJECT WOULD SILENTLY UNLATCH IT. Nothing would fail: this clause would simply stop matching the row it wrote, every sweep would find the group again, and one reminder stage (§G.9.1) would become a nag every sweep interval — the ladder oto refuses, built by accident. If that allocation ever changes, this literal changes with it, in the same commit. Correct as written; do not \"generalise\" it.\n\n`7570090` deletes `alert_groups`, which moves that allocation BY DEFINITION. So under that ticket the latch must acquire a new key, chosen deliberately, or the sweep nags every 60 s with nothing failing. And the ruled replacement makes it worse rather than better: a conversation keyed to one Case is stable where a generation was not, so a latch keyed on it would fire once and then suppress the reminder for every LATER incident in that conversation — a silent miss rather than a silent nag, which is the worse direction.\n\nRemoving the feature removes the question. **Do this before `7570090` stage 4, not after.**\n\n**Why it matters.** ⭐ THE SEQUENCING IS THE WHOLE VALUE OF THIS TICKET. Landed first, stage 4 never has to invent a latch key for a feature that is going away, and nobody has to get that choice right under pressure on the largest change in the branch. Landed after, or not at all, the branch carries a `NOT EXISTS` whose own comment says it fails silently in both directions.\n\nThere is a second reason the timing is favourable. `2078a07` records that the `@`-mention half of this feature has **never been observed working** — Amendment 3 ships `unacked_reminder_mention` on the strength of Slack's documentation alone, and Amendment 4 is the standing proof that Slack's documentation can be half wrong. Removing the feature retires an unverified surface rather than a proven one.\n\n**Where.** 71 files mention `unacked_reminder`/`UnackedReminder` outside `docs-site` and `node_modules`, so the list below is the spine rather than the whole:\n`internal/notification/repository/reminders.go` (`unackedGroupsSQL`, the latch, and the four-predicate liveness clause whose header `51f30bf` corrected) · `internal/notification/domain/reason.go` (`ReasonUnackedReminder` and its subject allocation) · `internal/app/workers.go` (the periodic) · the settings key `unacked_reminder_after_s` across `internal/identity/{domain,api,repository}` and `orgs.settings` · the three mention keys `unacked_reminder_mention`, `_list`, `_min_severity` (`internal/identity/repository/orgs.go:59-61`) · `web/src/features/settings/tuningCopy.ts` and `web/src/features/notifications/vocabulary.ts` · `deploy/helm/oto/values.yaml` · `api/openapi/openapi.yaml` and the generated client · SPEC §G.9.1 (the one-stage clause), §G.3's `notify.digest`-adjacent periodic row, and ADR 0020 Amendment 3.\n\n**Scope.** large. Not because any one edit is hard, but because it is a settings-contract removal, an API removal, a Reason retirement and a worker removal at once — and `27a1860` already established this repo's rule that deleting a settings key is a contract change of its own.\n\n⚠️ THE REASON IS RETIRED, NOT DELETED, AND THIS IS THE ONE CLAUSE NOT TO GET WRONG. `notifications.reason` holds up to 13 months of rows. Follow the `00051`/`00054` bargain the way `EventType.Retired()` still does: the constant stays, stays decodable, stays in the persisted-spellings expansion, and the writer refuses it. `a51a763` clause 5 asked for exactly this for `storm`, the branch did the opposite, and the consequence — a migration that 23514s against any database holding the old value — was acceptable only because the owner confirmed oto has never run anywhere real. Do not spend that concession twice.\n\n**Done when.** No sweep can mint an `unacked_reminder`, and `reminders.go`'s latch is gone rather than re-keyed. `ReasonUnackedReminder` is RETIRED — decodable, refused at the writer, present in the persisted-spellings expansion — and `notifications_reason_ck` is NOT narrowed to exclude it. The settings key and the three mention keys leave `AllSettingKeys`, the bounds table, the DTOs, `orgs.settings` (with a migration), `tuningCopy.ts` and the Helm values TOGETHER, and the declarative layer's boot refusal is handled explicitly for a values file that still names one, so an upgrade is a warning rather than a crash loop. SPEC §G.9.1 records that the one stage is now zero and why, ADR 0020 Amendment 3 is amended rather than left standing, and a superseding note says the mention surface was withdrawn before it was ever observed to work. `2078a07`'s mention checklist item is struck.","files":null}]}